          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which set of clinical features best distinguishes encephalitis from meningitis?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which set of clinical features best distinguishes encephalitis from meningitis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fever and headache</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fever and headache are common to both meningitis and encephalitis and cannot reliably distinguish between them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neck stiffness and Kernig's sign</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These are meningeal signs — characteristic of meningitis, where the meninges are inflamed. In encephalitis, the brain parenchyma itself is the primary site of inflammation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Altered consciousness, seizures, and focal neurological deficits</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Encephalitis involves inflammation of the brain parenchyma, producing altered consciousness, seizures, behavioural changes, and focal deficits — contrasting with the meningeal signs (neck stiffness, Kernig's, Brudzinski's) that dominate in meningitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Petechial rash and purpura</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Petechial rash is associated specifically with meningococcal meningitis (N. meningitidis bacteraemia), not with encephalitis. It reflects meningococcal septicaemia rather than brain parenchymal inflammation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: meningitis = meningeal signs dominant (neck stiffness, Kernig's, Brudzinski's); encephalitis = parenchymal signs dominant (altered consciousness, seizures, focal deficits, behavioural changes)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! The parenchymal signs of encephalitis versus the meningeal signs of meningitis is a fundamental clinical distinction you will use in emergency settings."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
